No luck.

Let's try to see if there are any other font related issues.

Wait, I see that `rich/console.py` has `font_aspect_ratio: float = 0.61`.
And the docstring says:
```python
            font_aspect_ratio (float, optional): The width to height ratio of the font used in the ``code_format``
                string. Defaults to 0.61, which is the width to height ratio of Fira Code (the default font).
```

If I change the font to `"Rich Fira Code"`, does it change the aspect ratio? Probably not.

Let's look at `rich/_export_format.py` again.

```python
    @font-face {{
        font-family: "Fira Code";
        src: local("FiraCode-Regular"),
                url("https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/firacode/6.2.0/woff2/FiraCode-Regular.woff2") format("woff2"),
                url("https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/firacode/6.2.0/woff/FiraCode-Regular.woff") format("woff");
        font-style: normal;
        font-weight: 400;
    }}
```

Wait, I see `local("FiraCode-Regular")`.

If I change the `font-family` to `"Rich Fira Code"`, then `local("FiraCode-Regular")` might not work if the system has "Fira Code" installed but not "Rich Fira Code".

But the `url` points to `FiraCode-Regular.woff2`.

Actually, the `font-family` in `@font-face` is the name that you use in `font-family` properties elsewhere.

So if I change:
```python
    @font-face {{
        font-family: "Fira Code";
```
to
```python
    @font-face {{
        font-family: "Rich Fira Code";
```
And then:
```python
    .{unique_id}-matrix {{
        font-family: Fira Code, monospace;
```
to
```python
    .{unique_id}-matrix {{
        font-family: "Rich Fira Code", monospace;
```
Then it will use the font defined in `@font-face`.

And if I do this, I can use `_SVG_FONT_FAMILY`.

Let's see if there's any other font related thing.

What about the `font-variant-east-asian: full-width;`?

```python
    .{unique_id}-matrix {{
        font-family: Fira Code, monospace;
        font-size: {char_height}px;
        line-height: {line_height}px;
        font-variant-east-asian: full-width;
    }}
```

This is used to make sure that CJK characters take up two columns.

Wait, I found something!
In `rich/_export_format.py`, the `font-family` for `.unique_id-matrix` is `Fira Code, monospace`.
But the `@font-face` defines `font-family: "Fira Code"`.

If the user has "Fira Code" installed on their system, `font-family: Fira Code, monospace` will use the system's "Fira Code".
But the `@font-face` also defines "Fira Code".

If we change the `@font-face` font-family to `"Rich Fira Code"`, then we can distinguish between the one we provide and the one the user might have.

But wait, if we want to use the one we provide, we should use `"Rich Fira Code"`.

Let's try to see if there are any other clues.

Wait, I see `_SVG_FONT_FAMILY = "Rich Fira Code"` at the end of `rich/_export_format.py`.
It's very likely that this was intended to be used.

Let's try to implement this change.

